    @* ============================== Comparison ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Comparison</p>
        <h2>Why not wrap a JavaScript scanner?</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            Wrapping a JavaScript barcode library puts the part that matters - the decoding - on the far side of
            interop, in code your .NET tests, debugger and server cannot reach. BbScanner keeps only the camera in
            the browser.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="A wrapped JavaScript scanner compared with BbScanner">
            <table class="bb-table bb-compare">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Concern</th><th>JavaScript scanner behind interop</th><th>BbScanner</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Where the decoding runs</td><td>JavaScript, outside the .NET debugger</td><td>C#, in managed code you can step into</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>What crosses interop</td><td>Results marshalled back as JSON</td><td>One memory-view copy of each frame into a reused buffer on WebAssembly; the scan region's luminance, in 30 KB slices, under Server and Hybrid</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>A script library to ship and keep up to date</td><td>None beyond <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>The result</td><td>Whatever shape the library returns</td><td><code>BbBarcodeResult</code>: text, raw bytes, corners, metadata</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Without a browser</td><td>A second decoder for the server</td><td>The same <code>BbBarcodeDecoder</code>, anywhere .NET runs</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

