    @* ============================== FAQ ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Questions</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <div class="bb-faq">
            <details>
                <summary>Which render modes can open the camera?</summary>
                <p>
                    All the interactive ones, on .NET 8, 9 and 10: a standalone Blazor WebAssembly app, Blazor Server,
                    the <code>InteractiveServer</code>, <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> and <code>InteractiveAuto</code>
                    pages of a Blazor Web App, and a Blazor Hybrid app's <code>BlazorWebView</code> in MAUI, WPF or
                    Windows Forms. The page always opens the camera; on WebAssembly the frames are decoded in the browser,
                    while under Server and Hybrid the page sends the scan region's luminance to .NET on the server or in
                    the app.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Why does the camera not start on my test server?</summary>
                <p>
                    Browsers only expose the camera in a secure context - a page served over HTTPS, or from
                    <code>localhost</code>. Anywhere else the component reports <code>NotSupported</code> straight away,
                    with a message that says so, rather than waiting on a prompt that will never appear. The render mode
                    makes no difference here; in a Blazor Hybrid app the platform has to allow the camera as well.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>How does it avoid misreads?</summary>
                <p>
                    Misreading is treated as worse than missing: check digits and guard patterns are verified, a
                    Reed-Solomon correction is only accepted when the corrected block re-verifies, and ITF, Codabar,
                    UPC-E, the GS1 DataBar family and unchecked Code 39 results must repeat on a second scan line
                    before they are reported. Narrowing <code>Formats</code> to what you expect, and
                    <code>ItfLengths</code> to the lengths you use, closes the rest of the gap.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it read PDF417?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes - the kind on driving licences, boarding passes and shipping labels. The library ships the
                    specification's symbol character table, finds a symbol at any rotation before reading it row by
                    row, and repairs damaged rows with the symbol's own error correction. Compact (truncated) PDF417,
                    with its right row indicator dropped, reads too; MicroPDF417, a different symbology, is not read.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it read Aztec?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes - the code on boarding passes and rail and transit tickets, compact and full range, at any
                    rotation and seen at an angle. Its bullseye is found wherever it is in the frame, and a large symbol
                    is fitted through its own reference grid. Mirrored symbols read too, and an Aztec Rune reads as its number.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it read MaxiCode and Micro QR?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes. MaxiCode, the hexagonal code with a bullseye on parcels, is found by its rings at any angle and
                    seen at a slant, and a carrier's structured message - postal code, country and class of service -
                    comes back as the carrier writes it. Micro QR, the one-finder QR for small parts, reads in all four
                    sizes and is only looked for when no QR code was, so it costs QR scanning nothing.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it read GS1 DataBar?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes, the whole family. Omnidirectional, Truncated and their two-row Stacked forms - the small
                    barcode on loose produce - and DataBar Limited, on packs too small for anything else, come back as
                    the GS1 element string, <code>01</code> and fourteen digits. DataBar Expanded and Expanded Stacked,
                    on weighed fresh food and coupons, come back as whatever element string they carry - a GTIN with its
                    weight, price, batch or best-before date - with a group separator after each variable length element.
                    A symbol too short, or turned too far, for one scan line to cross whole is pieced together from
                    several. The linear part of a GS1 Composite symbol is not read, because the two-dimensional part
                    that holds the rest of its data is not.
                </p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </section>

